**Read:** [Breviary Technical Ceramics — Verband der Keramischen Industrie](../sources/Breviary-Technical-Ceramics.lingua#r_7998JE61RKQF7VXXYK197KRBH4), manufacturing from powders; [Materials Processing — MIT 3.044, Spring 2013](../sources/Materials-Processing-MIT-3044.lingua#r_5RCMWE5WA6D0WS1ETHWRZVXMFR), powder and colloid lectures; the shared metallurgy powder Record.

Particle size is defined by a measurement and weighting: number, area and volume distributions can describe the same powder differently. Shape, agglomeration, moisture, oxide layers and binder alter flow, packing and reaction. A primary particle size is not the size of every agglomerate entering a die or slurry.

**Work:** Compare two reported powder distributions after reconciling definitions and sampling. Relate surface-to-volume scaling to impurity and binder demand. Describe how sieving, milling, drying or recycling could change chemistry as well as size. Select measurements for both incoming powder and a later compact, and explain why a supplier's mean diameter cannot by itself establish processability or final density.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
